# Presentations

Read the guidance relevant to each part of the request; a user may ask you to explain, edit and create in the same task. If they only ask questions about a deck, inspect it without making changes. Before editing an existing deck or following a template, read [template following](references/template_following.md). When creating or changing slide layouts, read [visual design guidance](style_guidelines.md). For Google Slides, read [Google Slides routing](routing/google_slides.md) first.

Proceed when the request is clear. Ask only when missing information would change what you make or how you make it.

## Artifact Template Selection

When creating new presentations without a template, reference, or visual direction, or when the user asks to browse or upload templates, read [template selection](references/template-elicitation.md) before choosing a design or starting creation.

## Writing Quality Instructions

Oftentimes, when an AI model makes slides, the writing is poor-quality and/or AI slop. You must instead write  in a clear, direct, and professional way for all content you write on the slide, following the instructions below.

The examples below show wording to avoid, why it is slop/bad writing, and how you would want to write that instead. They are non-exhaustive; apply the same judgment to writing quality more generally.

### Taxonomy

#### 1. Awkward/unnatural wording

Wording that seems unfit for the text and should be rephrased to seem more human.

| Don't write | Why it is slop/bad writing | You should instead write like this |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Word: â€œShopping has become more deliberate and **value-led**.â€ | â€œValue-ledâ€ is an awkward and vague phrase that does not carry much substance. It comes off as an empty abstraction. | â€œShopping has become more deliberate and focused on **value**â€ |
| Word: â€œContributions **waned** over the period.â€ | â€œWanedâ€ is unnecessary. It is more direct and intuitive to simply say â€œdecreasedâ€. | â€œContributions **decreased** over the periodâ€ |
| Word: â€œA **closer** 2021 **step-up** from a small **base**â€ | The word â€œcloserâ€ followed by â€œstep-upâ€ makes it unclear what the increase is â€˜closerâ€™ to. The comparison sounds unnatural and can be made more explicit by replacing it with â€œincreasedâ€. Baseline is a more natural phrasing of â€œbaseâ€. | â€œA **larger increase** in 2021 from a **low baseline**â€ |
| Phrase: â€œA **five-question pulse** uses simple 0-10 scores and one **verbatim follow-up** to reveal what matters most.â€ | The â€œfive-question pulseâ€ and â€œverbatim follow-upâ€ are unnatural phrasings that are difficult to parse. | â€œThe **five-question survey** uses a 0-10 scale and one **open-ended question**.â€ |
| Phrase: â€œSony calculates Adjusted OIBDA as operating income plus depreciation and amortization expense, **less profit and loss amounts** that Sony **deems non-recurring**.â€ | This phrasing is clunky and hard to read because it stacks multiple awkward phrases together. The phrase â€œdeems non-recurringâ€ is also awkward given that you could simply use non-recurring as an adjective (Sonyâ€™s involvement implied earlier in the sentence).<br><br>The phrase â€œless profit and loss amountsâ€ is also an awkward way to communicate the financial impact. | â€œSony calculates Adjusted OIBDA as operating income plus depreciation and amortization expenses,  **less non-recurring profits and losses**â€ |
| Phrase: â€œEvidence from the early COVID-19 through the inflationary aftermath shows consumers are **thinking harder before they buy.**â€ | â€œThinking harder before they buyâ€ is a very strange phrasing that should be rephrased to be more aligned with the text. | â€œSince COVID-19, consumers have become more **deliberate about purchases**â€ |
| Label: â€œImmediate demand shockâ€ | The word â€œimmediateâ€ unnecessarily repeats the suddenness of â€œshock,â€ which makes the statement feel awkward and redundant. | â€œSharp decline in demandâ€ |
| Label: â€œVERBATIM FOLLOW-UPâ€ | What is â€œverbatim follow-upâ€? That is an awkward/unnatural phrase that makes the underlying meaning unclear. | â€œOPEN-ENDED QUESTIONâ€ |
| Label: â€œPOST-COVID CONSUMERâ€ | This is supposed to mean consumers after COVID-19, but it's expressed awkwardly as an adjective. | â€œConsumers after COVID-19â€ |

#### 2. Excessive/flowery wording

Wording that is excessively elaborate, sophisticated-sounding, or slogan-like.

| Don't write | Why it is slop/bad writing | You should instead write like this |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Word: â€œIntroduced Snapdragon 8 Elite, pairing the custom Oryon CPU with stronger AI performance for **next-generation** flagship smartphones.â€ | â€œNext-generationâ€ is excessive and flowery because â€˜flagshipâ€™ already conveys that smartphones are advanced. | Omit the word entirely. |
| Phrase: â€œA **five-question pulse** uses simple 0-10 scores and one **verbatim follow-up** to reveal what matters most.â€ | The â€˜five-question pulseâ€™ and â€˜verbatim follow-upâ€™ are also unnecessarily wordy, and can be simplified to a less sophisticated-sounding phrase. | â€œThe **five-question survey** uses a 0-10 scale and one **open-ended follow-up.**â€ |
| Phrase: â€œTranslate priorities into **coordinated execution**.â€ | This phrase is flowery because it sounds action-oriented and polished but conveys little meaning. The words â€œcoordinated executionâ€ are overly abstract and excessive. | â€œTranslate priorities into **a clear execution plan**â€ |
| Phrase: â€œA **structurally lower** commute base.â€ | â€œStructurally lowerâ€  is inflated and overly elaborate, which obscures the intended meaning of the sentence. | â€œA **permanent** drop in commutingâ€ |
| Phrase: â€œThe organization seeks to **holistically cultivate a multifaceted ecosystem of synergistic capabilities** that meaningfully facilitates the **realization of enhanced outcomes**.â€ | â€œHolistically cultivate a multifaceted ecosystem of synergistic capabilitiesâ€ and â€œrealization of enhanced outcomesâ€ are excessively verbose and elaborate. It can be streamlined to convey the phraseâ€™s underlying message in a less sophisticated-sounding way. | â€œThe organization seeks to build a **cohesive set of capabilities** that will **improve its outcomes**â€ |
| Phrase: â€œReduced overhead through **scalable spans and layers**â€ | â€œScalable spans and layersâ€ is jargon-filled and adds little meaning. From the phrase, it is also unclear what â€œscalableâ€ is referring to. | â€œReduced overhead through **streamlined** **management structures**â€ |
| Label: â€œCatalyzing Transformative Excellenceâ€ | â€œCatalyzing transformative excellenceâ€ inflates a simple concept and makes the label sound overly sophisticated. | â€œPerformance Improvementâ€ |

#### 3. Vague/empty wording

Wording that is unclear, abstract, or general in a way that does not convey any real substance.

| Don't write | Why it is slop/bad writing | You should instead write like this |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Word: â€œFY24 revenue $2.68B (+26%), with **broad-based** usage across products.â€ | â€œBroad-basedâ€ is vague because it does not identify which products saw the increase in usage. It should be replaced with a more specific metric of growth to reflect the increase in usage. | â€œFY24 revenue $2.68B (+26%), with usage **increasing** across products.â€ |
| Phrase: â€œExecution depends on teams that deliver reliably, **sustain the core** and continuously improve operationsâ€ | â€œSustain the coreâ€ is vague phrasing that makes little sense. The jargon-heavy language needs to be replaced with a more specific representation of what the intended message is, clarifying that it is the core business. | â€œExecution depends on teams that deliver reliably, **sustain the core business**, and continuously improve operations.â€ |
| Phrase: â€œ**Progressive promotion** improves trust and usabilityâ€ | â€œProgressive promotionâ€ is vague and ambiguous, with no discernible meaning.  To make the phrase less abstract and slogan-like, it needs to mention exactly what is improving â€œtrust and usabilityâ€. | â€œ**Customer rewards** programs improve trust and usabilityâ€ |
| Phrase: â€œ**More consistent coverage** and **stronger transparency**â€ | â€œMore consistentâ€ and â€œstronger transparencyâ€ are vague and abstract phrases.<br><br>Given the slideâ€™s focus on operational improvements, the phrase should specify the concrete outcome, like more consistent staffing coverage and clearer scheduling visibility. | â€œ**Improved staffing coverage and scheduling visibility**â€ |
| Phrase: â€œ**Price and availability** lead **transportation adoption**â€ | Both â€œprice and availabilityâ€ and â€œtransportation adoptionâ€ are clear, but taken together convey no meaning. A specific causal relationship should be added to relay the two. | â€œPrice and availability are **the main drivers of** transport adoptionâ€ |
| Label: â€œStrategic Value Driversâ€ | â€œStrategic Value Driversâ€ is too abstract to identify what the section actually conveys. Given the slideâ€™s focus on revenue metrics, it should be more tailored to that. | â€œRevenue Metricsâ€ |

#### 4. Unnecessary wording

Wording that is wholly unnecessary and would be best to remove.

| Don't write | Why it is slop/bad writing | You should instead write like this |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Word: â€œ**Practical** consumer needs consistently outweigh broader supporting factors across Germany, the US, and China.â€ | â€œPracticalâ€ is unnecessary because â€œconsumer needsâ€ already suggests that they are concrete needs. Removing the word helps clarify the sentenceâ€™s intended meaning. | â€œConsumer needs consistently outweigh broader supporting factors across Germany, the US, and Chinaâ€ |
| Word: â€œOperating earnings after **defined** non-GAAP adjustmentsâ€ | â€œDefinedâ€ does not clarify anything. It makes the sentence more formal and obscures its initial meaning. | â€œOperating earnings after the non-GAAP adjustmentsâ€ |
| Phrase: â€œA five-question pulse uses simple 0-10 scores and one verbatim follow-up to **reveal what matters most.**â€ | The â€œreveal what matters mostâ€ is empty phrasing that does not affect the phraseâ€™s meaning when removed. | â€œThe five-question survey uses a 0-10 scale and one open-ended follow-up.â€ |
| Label: â€œA NEW ERAâ€ | Has no clear connection to the slideâ€™s discussion of employee survey results and does not communicate a specific point. | Omit the phrase entirely. |
| Label: â€œMOMENTUM AHEADâ€ | Arbitrary positive-sounding phrase that is not supported by or relevant to the refinancing information on the slide. | Omit the phrase entirely. |
| Label: â€œUNLOCKING POTENTIALâ€ | Generic promotional language that adds nothing to a slide showing transit recovery trends. | Omit the phrase entirely. |

#### 5. Illogical wording

Wording that is not coherent or logical and does not make sense.

| Don't write | Why it is slop/bad writing | You should instead write like this |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Word: â€œTest, **learn**, and scale the most promising ideas with disciplined investmentâ€ | While â€œtestingâ€ and â€œscalingâ€ promising ideas is coherent, â€œlearningâ€ promising ideas makes no sense. The context implies that this may instead be â€œdevelopmentâ€ of these ideas. | â€œ**Develop**, test, and scale the most promising ideas with disciplined investmentâ€ |
| Phrase: â€œTransit & **ground passenger** jobsâ€ | â€œTransit & ground passenger jobsâ€ is incomprehensible, especially because â€œground passenger jobsâ€ makes no logical sense. Fixing the linkage makes this coherent again. | â€œTransit and passenger transportation jobsâ€ |
| Phrase: â€œ**Incubate adjacent offerings** and business models that can **expand the core.**â€ | The dense phrasing of â€œincubate adjacent offeringsâ€ carries no meaning. The phrase â€œexpand the coreâ€ also not coherent because it doesnâ€™t specify what the â€˜coreâ€™ is referring to. | â€œDevelop **related products and business models** that expand the core business.â€ |
| Phrase: â€œ**New mobility concepts** and changing behavior in cities.â€ | â€œNew mobility conceptsâ€ is incoherent and does not make sense in the context of this sentence. The underlying message revolves around new transportation options, not novel â€œmobility conceptsâ€. | â€œNew transportation options and changing travel behavior in cities.â€ |
| Phrase: â€œ79% Transit use **back to pre-pandemic**â€ | The phrasing is not parseable. It could mean that transit use has returned to 79% of its pre-pandemic level, or that 79% represents the pre-pandemic transit-use rate. To make the relationship logical, it should state what the current level is and how it compares with the previous pre-pandemic levels. | â€œTransit use is at 79%, matching pre-pandemic levelsâ€<br><br>or<br><br>â€œTransit use has recovered to 79% of its pre-pandemic level.â€ |
| Label: â€œRevenue Growth Decline Expansionâ€ | â€œRevenue growth decline expansionâ€ is illogical because it combines contradictory ideas without explaining how they relate. â€œGrowthâ€ and â€œdeclineâ€ point in different directions. | â€œRevenue Declineâ€ |

|  | Words | Phrases | Labels |
