# Visual design

Use this guidance when creating or changing slide layouts. Follow the user’s template and design instructions first.

## Design for the content

Keep the template's fonts, colors, spacing, images and repeated elements. When the user has not specified a design, choose consistent styles and layouts that suit the content. Use bullets, rows of text, diagrams, tables or columns as needed; no single layout is required.

Use editable tables for data arranged in rows and columns, and charts to show relationships in data. Align related items, place labels near what they describe and keep connecting lines clear of text. Use emphasis to draw attention to important information. Follow the default-styling rules in [SKILL.md's Important instructions](SKILL.md#important-instructions). Only use UI-like elements when the user explicitly requests them or when matching a supplied template requires them; otherwise use a flat slide composition rather than repeated UI panels.

Make text readable at the size the audience will see it. Use one font familywith sizes and weights that distinguish headings from body text. Use available fonts that contain all required characters. Match reference fonts where specified instead of replacing them with defaults. Check line wrapping, padding inside text boxes and the space bold text needs. Before shrinking crowded text, try clearer wording or a better arrangement without dropping required evidence.

Use images that accurately represent the subject. By default, do not reuse the same image more than once, except for backgrounds. Preserve repetition explicitly required by the user or a supplied template. Crop decorative images to fit the layout, but do not crop away evidence or distort logos. Do not present generated illustrations as photos of real events or images of real objects.

Use diagrams when they help explain or reinforce the narrative of the slide. If using arrows, orient shapes such that you can avoid diagonal or curved connections.

Use the fewest distinct text groups the content needs. It is unacceptable to clutter slides with unnecessary kickers, section labels, and footer fragments. e.g. instead of a textbox that says "The supplement requires separate authority", "FY2027 inflation sensitivity: 2% gives $3578m, 4% gives $364.2," on one line, then "02 FY2027 Planning Estimate" and "Reporting through 2025" in opposite corners of the footer, just have ONE footnote saying "2% inflation rate assumption in FY2027 drives up operating cost to $357.8M and 4% inflation rate yields $364.2M. Note reporting is through 2025 and actual budget approval requires separate approval from leadership." Kill any extraneous elements that are obvious from the deck context

## Visual review
Review the slide order and the balance of dense and simple slides. Then inspect each slide at a consistent readable size. Check that required content is present, the main point is easy to find, text is readable, and images, labels and other objects fit without unintended overlap. For template work, compare source and output side by side. Keep the original style when the user asked to preserve it.
# Task: Create / edit a DOCX

## Default tool: python-docx
Use `python-docx` for:
- paragraphs/runs
- built-in styles (Title / Heading 1 / Heading 2)
- tables (structure + cell text + basic formatting)
- simple headers/footers and margins

## Practical python-docx gotchas

### 1) Header/footer tables require a width
When adding tables to headers/footers, `add_table` requires an explicit width:

```python
from docx.shared import Inches
from docx.enum.text import WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH

section = doc.sections[0]
footer = section.footer
table = footer.add_table(rows=1, cols=3, width=Inches(6.5))
# Align text inside each cell
table.rows[0].cells[0].paragraphs[0].alignment = WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.LEFT
```

### 2) Fonts can require setting both `run.font.name` and `w:rFonts`
Some renderers/Word builds don’t respect only `run.font.name`:

```python
from docx.oxml.ns import qn

run.font.name = "Gill Sans"
run._element.rPr.rFonts.set(qn("w:ascii"), "Gill Sans")
run._element.rPr.rFonts.set(qn("w:hAnsi"), "Gill Sans")
```

### 3) “Clear header paragraph” isn’t always one call
If you need to replace an existing header paragraph, remove runs (or replace the paragraph XML). Avoid assuming a `clear()` method exists.

### 4) Tracked changes and comments are not first-class
If the user requests *real* tracked changes or *real* Word comments, plan for OOXML patching (see `ooxml/`).

## After every meaningful batch of edits: render and review
Use the loop from `tasks/verify_render.md` (DOCX → PNG) to avoid shipping layout defects. (Internally the renderer uses a PDF step; `--emit_pdf` can persist it if needed.)

## Output hygiene
Keep `/mnt/data` clean: deliverables only unless the user asks for intermediate render artifacts.
# Task: Verify / render a DOCX (DOCX → PNG)

## Why this exists
DOCX editing tools can "succeed" while the visual output is broken. Always verify by rendering.

## Preferred: use the packaged renderer
This uses a dedicated LibreOffice profile + writable HOME and produces `page-<N>.png` images:

```bash
python render_docx.py /mnt/data/input.docx --output_dir /mnt/data/out
# For debugging LibreOffice failures:
python render_docx.py /mnt/data/input.docx --output_dir /mnt/data/out --verbose
# Optional: also write <input_stem>.pdf to --output_dir (for debugging/archival):
python render_docx.py /mnt/data/input.docx --output_dir /mnt/data/out --emit_pdf
```

## Manual render command (if you need it)
Use a unique LibreOffice profile (permission/locking issues are common in containers):

```bash
OUTDIR=/mnt/data/out
INPUT=/mnt/data/input.docx
BASENAME=$(basename "$INPUT" .docx)
LO_PROFILE=/mnt/data/.lo_profile_${BASENAME}_$$
mkdir -p "$OUTDIR" "$LO_PROFILE"

HOME="$LO_PROFILE" soffice --headless -env:UserInstallation=file://"$LO_PROFILE" \
  --convert-to pdf --outdir "$OUTDIR" "$INPUT"

pdftoppm -png "$OUTDIR/$BASENAME.pdf" "$OUTDIR/$BASENAME"
```

## Success criteria
- PNGs exist for each page
- Spot-check page count and representative pages

**Note:** LibreOffice sometimes prints scary-looking stderr (e.g., `error : Unknown IO error`) even when output is correct. Treat the conversion as successful if the PNGs exist and look correct (and if you used `--emit_pdf`, the PDF exists and is non-empty).

## What to check in the PNGs
- clipped text (especially headings and table cells)
- overlapping objects
- broken tables (wrapping, misalignment, missing borders)
- unexpected font substitution
- header/footer alignment and page breaks

## Caveats
- **Comments often don’t render** in headless LibreOffice PDFs. Use structural checks for comments.
- **Field codes (page numbers, TOC)** may show placeholder values in some PDF renders. If the user needs proof, re-check in Word or update fields before final render.
- **Multi-section docs** can have different page sizes/orientations; DPI is computed from the first section by default. If some pages look scaled oddly, use `--dpi` to override.

## Delivery checklist
- Final DOCX is clean (no internal citation tokens, no placeholder text)
- Final render looks correct on all pages
- `/mnt/data` contains only final outputs (unless user asked for intermediates)

